      },
      {
        "elements": [
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 4.415011037527593,
            "width": 37.98882681564246,
            "height": 8.830022075055187,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><span style=\"font-size:1.5em;\"><strong>INFORMATION TEXT (Part 2)</strong></span></p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "55aaf4d3-f7e3-4a3e-9196-01a6f7474f94"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          },
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582782,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Relationship with Other Composers</strong><br>\nSchubert's life overlapped with that of Beethoven, whom he revered greatly. Although they inhabited the same city and shared acquaintances, there is no concrete evidence that they had a significant personal relationship. Schubert admired Beethoven's work immensely, and it had a lasting influence on his own compositions.</p>\n\n<p><strong>The \"Schubertiades\"</strong><br>\nSchubert's music became the centerpiece of intimate gatherings known as \"Schubertiades,\" where his circle of friends and patrons would come together to enjoy his latest works. These events reflected the growing trend of smaller, private concerts rather than large public performances. They were crucial in cultivating a dedicated following for Schubert's music.</p>\n\n<p><strong>The Breadth of Schubert's Work</strong><br>\nSchubert's compositions cover a vast array of styles and forms, including over 600 lieder, seven completed symphonies, operas, chamber music, and piano works. His \"Unfinished Symphony\" remains one of his most famous pieces, notable for its haunting melodies and unfinished status, leaving listeners to ponder what could have been.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "3b13867b-feab-4e2e-bfa2-fcc82e4dbff1"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
