          {
            "x": 2.178649237472767,
            "y": 12.910611231371064,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Signature Musical Style</strong><br>\nSchubert is known for his distinctive melodic style, which often expressed a profound depth of feeling and melancholy. His use of harmony was innovative, and his melodies were incredibly lyrical, sometimes resembling the narrative and dramatic arc of a story, which made his songs particularly effective and enduring.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Late Works and Unfinished Pieces</strong><br>\nIn the final years of his life, Schubert composed some of his most profound music, including the \"Winterreise\" song cycle and the \"C Major Symphony.\" His late pieces, rich with emotion and depth, were not always understood by his contemporaries but have since been recognized as some of his greatest works.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Death and Posthumous Fame</strong><br>\nSchubert died at the age of 31, having suffered from poor health in the later years of his life, exacerbated by financial woes and the deprivation of the artist's life in Vienna. His reputation grew after his death as his work was posthumously published and performed. Today, Schubert is hailed as one of the leading figures of early Romantic music, with an enduring influence on the lied and other forms of music.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "3d655444-fd55-42f7-aba2-16a16db7dc58"
